content:
Well, at least they went quite a way. With Google Base one can create new types of entities, entities themselves, and search for them. I am not too sure about the User Interface yet, but it&rsquo;s surely one of the best actually running onbig amounts of data. Nice query refinement, really.
But heck, there&rsquo;s one thing that scares me off. I was looking today for all the people interested in the Semantic Web, and there are already some in. And you can filter them by gender. I was just gently surprised about the choices I was offered when I wanted to filter them by gender&hellip;
Oh come on, Google. I know there are not that many girls in computer science, but really, it&rsquo;s not that bad!

Sometimes I stumble upon posts that leave me wonder, what actually do people think about the whole Semantic Web idea, and about standards like RDF, OWL and the like. Do you think academia people went out and purposefully made them complicated? That they don&rsquo;t want them to get used?
Christopher St. John wrote down some nice experience with using RDF for logging. And he was pretty astonished that &ldquo;RDF can actually be a pretty nifty tool if you don&rsquo;t let it go to your head. And it worked great.&rdquo;
And then: &ldquo;Using RDF doesn&rsquo;t actually add anything I couldn&rsquo;t have done before, but it does mean I get to take advantage of tons of existing tools and expertise.&rdquo; Well, that&rsquo;s pretty much the point of standards. And the point of the whole Semantic Web idea. There won&rsquo;t be anything you will be able to do later, that you&rsquo;re not able to do today! You know, assembler was pretty turing-complete already. But having your data in standard formats helps you. &ldquo;You can buy a book on RDF, but you&rsquo;re never going to buy a book on whatever internal debug format you come up with&rdquo;
Stop being surprised that some things on the Semantic Web work. And don&rsquo;t expect miracles either.
Comments
Christopher St. John (11 October 2005 09:02)
You wonder &ldquo;what do people think of the whole Semantic Web idea?&rdquo;. Well&hellip;
I suffered through the 80&rsquo;s Knowledge Representation fad, both academically in the AI program at Edinburgh and as a practitioner at the only company ever to produce a commercial system written in Prolog (that wasn&rsquo;t a Prolog development system.) So I&rsquo;m familiar with the problems that the Semantic Web effort is attempting to address. Having slogged through real-life efforts to encode substantial amounts of knowledge, I find some of the misty-eyed musings that surround the Semantic Web effort depressing. That &ldquo;most information on the Web is designed for human consumption&rdquo; is seen as an obstacle surmountable via tools like RDF is especially sad. On the other hand, I&rsquo;m always happy to make use of the cool tools that these sorts of things seem to throw off. There&rsquo;s probably a certain Proverbs 26:11 aspect to it as well.
Denny (20 October 2005 13:45)
I answered in the next post, Why some are disenchanted

Not everyone likes our proposal for the Semantic Wikipedia. That&rsquo;s not a big surprise really. Boris Mann was talking about the advantages of tagging, and some ideas like blessed tags, that sounded very nice, when Jay Fienberg pointed him to the Semantic MediaWiki proposal. Boris answers: &ldquo;I notice with a shudder however, that the Mediawiki stuff uses a large &ldquo;S&rdquo; Semantic, and includes RDF. I admit it, I&rsquo;m afraid of RDF.&rdquo;
Yes, we do. And we&rsquo;re proud of it. Actually, it&rsquo;s the base for the better half of the possible applications we describe. Jay has some nice answers to it: &ldquo;I think the MediaWiki folks are just recognizing the connection between their &ldquo;tags&rdquo; and the big &ldquo;S&rdquo; Semantic Web [you bet!, denny]. There are taxonomies and ontologies behind the popular tagging apps too&ndash;folks behind them just aren&rsquo;t recognizing / publicizing this (for a number of reasons, including that tags are often part of a practical application without big &ldquo;S&rdquo; Semantic Web goals). [&hellip;] I&rsquo;m not a super huge fan of RDF myself, but I think it&rsquo;s useful to not be afraid of it, because some interesting things will come out of it at some point.&rdquo;
Our idea was to allow the user to use Semantic Web technologies even without really understanding them. No one needs to understand RDF fully, or OWL, to be able to use it. Sure, if she does, well, it surely will help her. Any by the way, RDF really is not complicated at all, it just has a syntax that sucks. So what?
Maybe it&rsquo;s a crude joke of history to start the Semantic Web with syntactic problems&hellip;
By the way, does anyone have a spare invitation to GMail for me? I&rsquo;d really like to check out their service. Thanks, Peter, that was fast.
Comments
Peter Herndon (7 September 2005 11:13)
My pleasure. I&rsquo;ve got, evidently, 99 more, BTW&hellip;

This day no theoretical talks, but instead two invited speakers - and much social programme, with a lunch at a swimming pool and a dinner in Segovia. Segovia is a beautiful town, with a huge, real, still standing roman aqueduct. Stunning. And there I ate the best pork ever! The aqueduct survived the huge earthquake of Lisbon of 1755, although houses around it crumbled and broke. This is, because it is built without any mortar - just stone over stone. So the stones could swing and move slightly, and the construction survived.
Made me think of loosely coupled systems. I probably had too much computer science the last few days.
The talks were very different today: first was Mike Woolridge of the University of Liverpool. He talked about Multiagent Systems in the past, the present and the future. He identified five trends in computing: Ubiquity, Interconnection, Intelligence, Delegation and Human-orientation.
His view on intelligence was very interesting: it is about the complexity of tasks that we are able to automate and delegate to computers. He quoted John Alan Robertson - the guy who invented resolution calculus, a professor of philosophy - as exclaiming &ldquo;This is Artificial Intelligence!&rdquo;, when he saw a presentation of the FORTRAN compiler at a conference. I guess the point was, don&rsquo;t mind about becoming as intelligent as humans, just mind at getting closer.
&ldquo;The fact that humans were in control of cars - our grandchildren will be quite uncomfortable with this idea.&rdquo;
The second talk was returning to the Semantic Web in a very pragmatic way: how to make money with it? Richard Benjamins of iSOCO just flew in from Amsterdam where he was at the SEKT meeting, and he brought promising news about the developing market for Semantic Web technologies. Mike Woolridge was criticizing Richard&rsquo;s optimistic projections and noted that he also, about ten years ago, spent a lot of energy and money into the growing Multiagent market - and lost most of it. It was an interesting discussion - Richard being the believer, Mike the sceptic, and a lot of young people betting a few years worth of life on the ideas presented by the first one&hellip;

Yeah, sure, the Summer School for the Semantic Web is over for quite a while now, and here I started to blog about it daily, and didn&rsquo;t manage to get over the first three days. Let&rsquo;s face it: it was too much! The program was so dense, the social events so enjoyable, I couldn&rsquo;t even spare half an hour a day to continue the blogging. Now I want to recap some of my notes and memories I have of the second half of the Summer School. My bad memory be damned - if you want to correct something feel free to do so.
This day&rsquo;s invited speaker was Roberto Basili of the University of Rome. He sketched the huge field of natural language processing, and although he illustrated the possible interactions between lexical knowledge bases and ontologies, he nevertheless made a strong distinction between these two. Words are not concepts. &ldquo;The name should have no value for defining a concept.&rdquo; This is like &ldquo;Don&rsquo;t look into URIs&rdquo; for HLT-people. He made a very interesting point: abductions will become very important in the Semantic Web, as they model human thinking patterns much closer than strict deduction does. Up until this day I was quite against abductions, I discussed this issue very stubbornly in Granada. But Roberto made me aware of a slightly different viewpoint: just sell abductive resolutions as suggestions, as proposals to the user
et voilà, the world is a better place! I will have to think abou this a bit more some day, but he did made me think. The theoretical sessions and workshops today were packed and strenuos: we jumped from annotations to Semantic Web Services and back again. Fabio Ciravegna of the University of Sheffield&rsquo;s NLP-Group, who created tools like Armadillo and GATE, gave us a thorough introduction to annotations for the Semantic Web and the usage of Human Language Technologies in order to enhance this task. He admitted that many of the tools are still quite unhandy, but he tried to make a point by saying: &ldquo;No one writes HTML today anymore with a text editor like Emacs or Notepad&hellip; or do you?&rdquo;
All students raised their hands. Yes, we do! &ldquo;Well, in the real world at least they don&rsquo;t&hellip;&rdquo;
He also made some critical comments on the developments of the Semantic Web: the technologies being developed right now allow for a today unknown ability of collecting and combining data. Does this mean, our technologies actually require a better world? One with no secrets, privacy and spam, because there is no need for such ideas? Is metadata just adding hay to the haystak instead of really finding the needle?
John Domingue&rsquo;s Talk on Semantic Web (Web) Services was a deep and profound introduction to the field, and especially to the IRS system developed by the KMi at Open University. He was defending WSMO valiantly, but due to time constraints pitily skipped the comparison with OWL-S. But he motivated the need for Semantic Web Services and sketched a possible solution.
The day ended in Cercedilla, where we besieged a local disco. I guess the people were hiding, &ldquo;watch it, them nerds are coming!&rdquo; ;) The music surprisingly old - they had those funny vinyl albums - but heck, Frank Sinatra is never outdated. But the 80s certainly are&hellip;

